  "designDoc": "## Scope and compatibility\n\nAdd persistent redirect events and a read-only analytics endpoint without changing existing successful response schemas or endpoint paths. Existing `Link.clickCount` remains the legacy counter returned by `LinkResponse`; analytics uses a separate event table and does not replace that counter. The existing controller -> service -> repository layering and constructor injection remain unchanged. All new persistence classes use `jakarta.persistence` and all validation remains at the controller boundary.\n\n## Components and responsibilities\n\n- `RedirectEvent`: JPA entity representing one successful redirect. It contains an autogenerated identifier, a `ManyToOne` association to `Link`, an `Instant occurredAt`, and a nullable raw `referrer` column. The referrer column is bounded to 2048 characters; values longer than this are rejected or safely truncated before persistence according to the implementation's existing input policy, but the value must not be logged.\n- `RedirectEventRepository`: Spring Data JPA repository scoped by link/code. It provides event insertion, all-time count, date-range retrieval or grouped daily counts, and grouped non-empty referrer counts. Queries must include the requested link/code so events for other links cannot contribute.\n- `LinkRepository`: retain `findByCode`, `existsByCode`, and the atomic `incrementClickCount` update. No existing method or response behavior is removed.\n- `UrlValidator`: retain all existing absolute HTTP/HTTPS validation and add configured-origin comparison. A destination is rejected with `InvalidUrlException` if its normalized scheme, normalized host, and effective port match the configured public origin.\n- `PublicOriginConfiguration` or equivalent configuration component: reads an optional `app.public-origin`. If absent, derives the origin from configured server settings (`server.ssl.enabled`, `server.address` where applicable, and `server.port`), defaulting to HTTP port 8080. It parses and validates the configured origin at startup; it never derives the comparison origin from an individual request.\n- `LinkService`: retains creation, lookup, and legacy click-count behavior. Its follow operation resolves the link, increments the legacy counter atomically, and persists one `RedirectEvent` containing `Instant.now(clock)` and the incoming Referer value.\n- `AnalyticsService`: verifies the link using `LinkService.getByCode`, calculates the UTC 30-day window, queries event data, fills zero-count dates, sorts referrers, limits to five, and constructs the response records.\n- `RedirectController`: reads `HttpServletRequest.getHeader(\"Referer\")` and passes it to the existing follow service. It continues to return HTTP 302 with the same `Location` header and empty body.\n- `LinkController`: retains create and details operations and adds `GET /api/links/{code}/analytics`. The exact analytics mapping is more specific than `/{code}`, so `/analytics` is dispatched to the analytics operation.\n- `GlobalExceptionHandler`: existing `InvalidUrlException` and `LinkNotFoundException` mappings are reused. No new error shape is introduced.\n\n## Request flows\n\n### Create link\n\n1. `POST /api/links` is deserialized and validated by Bean Validation.\n2. `UrlValidator.validate` applies the existing URL rules and then compares the destination origin with the configured public origin.\n3. A same-origin destination throws `InvalidUrlException`, producing the existing HTTP 400 ProblemDetail response and no persisted link.\n4. An external destination follows the existing collision-safe code generation loop and returns the unchanged 201 response, `Location` header, and `LinkResponse` body.\n\n### Retrieve link details\n\n1. `GET /api/links/{code}` calls the existing lookup.\n2. Unknown codes continue to produce the existing 404 ProblemDetail.\n3. Existing `LinkResponse` construction and short URL generation are unchanged.\n\n### Redirect\n\n1. `GET /{code}` resolves the code before recording anything.\n2. Unknown codes throw `LinkNotFoundException`; no event is inserted and the existing 404 response is returned.\n3. For a resolved link, the service atomically increments `Link.clickCount` and inserts one `RedirectEvent` in the same transaction. The event timestamp is an `Instant`; the Referer value is stored exactly as received when non-null and non-empty, otherwise as null.\n4. The controller returns the existing HTTP 302 response with the destination in `Location`. Analytics does not alter the redirect target, headers, or body.\n\nThe normal redirect operation is transactional. If persistence fails, the transaction rolls back and the request returns the existing generic server-error behavior rather than returning a redirect that was not recorded. This preserves consistency between the legacy counter and event data; a failed persistence operation must not be silently reported as a successful tracked redirect.\n\n### Analytics\n\n1. `GET /api/links/{code}/analytics` first calls the existing link lookup. Unknown codes therefore use the existing 404 status and ProblemDetail shape.\n2. The service obtains the current instant from an injectable `Clock` and converts it to UTC.\n3. The date window is `[todayUtc - 29 days, todayUtc]`, inclusive, and contains exactly 30 UTC `LocalDate` values in ascending order.\n4. `totalClicks` counts every event for the link, including events older than the 30-day window.\n5. Daily aggregation includes only events whose UTC date is within the window. Missing dates are emitted with zero.\n6. Referrer grouping excludes null and empty strings. It sorts by descending count and then lexicographically by the raw referrer string, and returns at most five values.\n\n## Data model\n\n`links` remains unchanged. Add a `redirect_events` table:\n\n- `id BIGINT` primary key, generated by the database.\n- `link_id BIGINT` non-null foreign-key association to `links.id`, indexed.\n- `occurred_at TIMESTAMP` non-null, storing the `Instant`, indexed with `link_id`.\n- `referrer VARCHAR(2048)` nullable.\n\nUse JPA `ddl-auto: update`, matching the current application. Existing links receive no backfilled events and consequently report zero analytics until new redirects occur. H2 remains the configured persistence mechanism; events have the same lifecycle and restart characteristics as links. The existing in-memory H2 configuration is not made durable by this feature.\n\nRecommended records:\n\n- `ClickCount(LocalDate date, long clicks)` serialized as `{ \"date\": \"YYYY-MM-DD\", \"clicks\": number }`.\n- `ReferrerCount(String referrer, long clicks)` serialized as `{ \"referrer\": string, \"clicks\": number }`.\n- `LinkAnalyticsResponse(long totalClicks, List<ClickCount> clicksPerDay, List<ReferrerCount> topReferrers)`.\n\n## Key algorithms\n\n### Collision-safe short-code creation\n\nRetain the existing 7-character `SecureRandom` generator and ten-attempt loop. Continue checking `existsByCode`, rely on the unique database constraint for races, catch `DataIntegrityViolationException`, and retry with a new code. Exhaustion continues to produce `CodeGenerationException` and HTTP 503.\n\n### Origin comparison\n\nParse the configured public origin once. Normalize scheme and host using lower case and compare effective ports: explicit HTTP 80 and HTTPS 443 are equivalent to omitted default ports. Reject every absolute HTTP or HTTPS destination with matching scheme, host, and effective port, regardless of path, query, or fragment. User information is not considered part of the origin and must not make a same-origin URL acceptable. A malformed public-origin configuration should fail application startup rather than disable loop protection. Destinations with a different origin continue through the existing validation rules.\n\n### Analytics aggregation\n\nPrefer database-side `count`, date-range retrieval/grouping, and referrer grouping queries. If database portability or H2 query limitations make grouping queries unsuitable, retrieve only events for the requested link and aggregate in Java. Never load events for unrelated links. Convert every `Instant` using `instant.atZone(ZoneOffset.UTC).toLocalDate()`. Generate dates by iterating from `todayUtc.minusDays(29)` through `todayUtc`, then map counts with zero defaults. Sort referrer results using descending count followed by natural lexicographic referrer ordering, then apply `limit(5)`.\n\n## Concurrency and reliability\n\n- `incrementClickCount` remains a database-side atomic update, preventing lost legacy counter increments under concurrent redirects.\n- Each redirect event is an independent inserted row, so concurrent redirects cannot overwrite one another.\n- The counter increment and event insertion execute in one transaction for a resolved code. Unknown codes perform neither operation.\n- The event table has indexes on `(link_id, occurred_at)` and `(link_id, referrer)` where supported by JPA/database configuration.\n- Analytics reads do not mutate links or events.\n- A fixed `Clock` is injected in unit tests; production uses `Clock.systemUTC()`.\n- Raw Referer values are not logged and are not returned except in the requested top-referrers response.\n- The implementation does not add external queues, caches, services, or dependencies.\n\n## Brownfield compatibility\n\nDo not rename or remove `Link`, `LinkResponse`, `LinkRepository`, existing mappings, or existing exception handlers. Do not change the existing `clickCount` meaning or response records. Add the event association separately rather than exposing it through `LinkResponse`. The only intentional behavior change to an existing operation is rejection of destinations matching the configured public origin. All other valid external URLs and all existing not-found, malformed-request, collision, retrieval, and redirect contracts remain unchanged.",
