{
  "summary": "The implementation correctly restricts destination schemes to HTTP/HTTPS, records redirect analytics, and does not expose credentials. However, production release should address unbounded inputs and analytics resource consumption, missing abuse controls for short-code endpoints, and incomplete public-origin configuration behind proxies or custom domains.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/UrlValidator.java",
      "issue": "The destination URL has no application-level maximum length. Arbitrarily large request bodies and URL strings are parsed and passed to persistence, where the 2048-character database limit may cause expensive processing or a generic server error. This provides an avoidable denial-of-service vector.",
      "recommendation": "Enforce a bounded URL length before parsing and persistence, aligned with the database column size, and reject oversized values using the existing invalid-URL response contract. Also configure an appropriate maximum HTTP request size."
    },
    {
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "file": "src/main/java/com/example/shortener/AnalyticsService.java",
      "issue": "Analytics queries load every redirect event for the requested link into application memory, and the service performs an additional full event query for referrer aggregation. Event volume is unbounded, so an attacker can generate redirects and then cause high memory, CPU, and database load by repeatedly requesting analytics.",
      "recommendation": "Perform total, daily, and top-referrer aggregation in database queries with bounded date predicates and GROUP BY/ORDER BY/LIMIT operations. Add suitable indexes and enforce operational limits or rate limits on analytics requests."
